1 Introduction
The parallel chip-firing game is an automaton on a finite, connected graph G⁡(V,E)G(V,\,E) that was first introduced by Bitar and Goles Bitar and Goles (1992) in 1992. A parallel chip-firing game begins with some chips distributed among each v∈Vv\in V. On each subsequent round, all vertices with at least as many chips as neighbors simultaneously fire one chip to each of their neighbors. All other vertices wait. As observed by Bitar and Goles Bitar and Goles (1992), all parallel chip-firing games are periodic with some minimal period length TT, because the quantity of chips and the number of vertices in any parallel chip-firing game are fixed.
The parallel chip-firing game on undirected graphs has been extensively studied for trees Bitar and Goles (1992), simple cycles Dall’Asta (2006), complete graphs Levine (2011), and complete bipartite graphs Jiang (2010). See Table 1 for the possible period lengths of parallel chip-firing games on these graphs. For general graphs, Kominers and Kominers Kominers and Kominers (2010) showed that T=1T=1 for all parallel chip-firing games with enough chips. Later, Bu, Choi, and Xu Bu et al. (2022) made this range of chips exact. Furthermore, Kiwi et al. Kiwi et al. (1994) showed that TT cannot be bounded by a polynomial in |V||V|. Lastly, Scully, Jiang, and Zhang Jiang et al. (2015) distinguished the possible atomic firing sequences — length-TT binary strings that indicate the rounds in which a vertex fires within a periodic sequence — of a vertex in a parallel chip-firing game.
In 1994, Prisner Prisner (1994) extended the parallel chip-firing game to directed graphs. Prisner Prisner (1994) found that T=1T=1 for all games on acyclic directed graphs and that there exists no polynomial bound on |V||V| for the period of a parallel chip-firing game on a directed graph. Later, Goles and Prisner Goles and Prisner (2000) found that T≤|V|T\leq|V| for all parallel chip-firing games on orientations of Kn¯∗H\overline{K_{n}}*H, where Kn¯\overline{K_{n}} is an empty graph, ∗* denotes the graph join operation, and HH is an arbitrary graph. Finally, Ndoundam, Tchuente, and Tadonki Ndoundam et al. (2010) characterized the possible periods on orientations of the nn-cube graph.
In this paper, we prove the analogs of results for the parallel chip-firing game on undirected graphs for those on directed graphs. In Section 2, we establish preliminaries. In Section 3, we characterize the possible periods of parallel chip-firing games on certain classes of directed graphs. In addition, we observe that while there always exists a parallel chip-firing game with T=2T=2 on any undirected graph, the same is not true for orientations of complete directed graphs with at most four vertices. In Section 4, we prove that a binary string ss can be the atomic firing sequence of a vertex in a parallel chip-firing game on a strongly connected directed graph if and only if ss contains 1 or s=0s=0. Finally, in Section 5, we present unsolved conjectures and areas for future study.
