2 Preliminaries
We define a directed graph D=(V,E)D=(V,\,E) as a set of vertices VV and a set of ordered pairs of vertices (edges) EE. The edge (u,v)(u,\,v) for u,v∈Vu,\,v\in V is said to be directed from uu to vv. We define an orientation of an undirected graph G=(V,E)G=(V,\,E) to be a directed graph D=(V,E′)D=(V,\,E^{\prime}) such that either (u,v)∈E′(u,\,v)\in E^{\prime} or (v,u)∈E′(v,\,u)\in E^{\prime} if and only if there exists an edge between uu and vv in GG, but there do not exist vertices uu and vv such that (u,v)(u,\,v) and (v,u)(v,\,u) are both elements in E′E^{\prime}. The in-degree of a vertex vv is denoted deg−⁡v\deg^{-}v and is the number of edges directed from another vertex to vv. The out-degree of a vertex vv is denoted deg+⁡v\deg^{+}v and is the number of edges directed from vv to another vertex. In the directed graph in Figure 1, deg+⁡v3=2\deg^{+}v_{3}=2 and deg−⁡v3=1\deg^{-}v_{3}=1. The degree of a vertex vv is denoted deg⁡v\deg v and is the total number of edges including vv. In Figure 1, deg⁡v3=3\deg v_{3}=3. The distance between two vertices uu and vv is the length of the shortest path from uu to vv and is denoted d⁡(u,v)d(u,\,v). In the undirected graph in Figure 1, d⁡(v1,v4)=2d(v_{1},\,v_{4})=2. In the directed graph, d⁡(v1,v4)=3d(v_{1},\,v_{4})=3. For parallel chip-firing games on directed graphs, a vertex fires when the number of chips it possesses exceeds its out-degree, upon which the vertex fires one chip across each outgoing edge.
