The following lemma shows that vertex 4 fires the most in all parallel chip-firing games on a useful orientation of Ka,aK_{a,\,a} with a≥3a\geq 3.
Lemma 3.14.
For any a≥3a\geq 3, all parallel chip-firing games on a useful orientation of Ka,aK_{a,\,a} satisfy f4≥fif_{4}\geq f_{i} for all 1≤i≤2​a1\leq i\leq 2a.
Proof.
Since deg+⁡v4=1\deg^{+}v_{4}=1, it must fire once in each period for every chip it receives in that period. Note that v4v_{4} receives chips from all vertices v2​i−1v_{2i-1} with 1≤i≤a1\leq i\leq a except v5v_{5}, so f4≥f2​i−1f_{4}\geq f_{2i-1} for all 1≤i≤a1\leq i\leq a. Also note that deg+⁡v3=1\deg^{+}v_{3}=1 and that v3v_{3} receives chips from all vertices v2​iv_{2i} with 1≤i≤a1\leq i\leq a and i≠2i\neq 2. Thus, f4≥f3≥f2​if_{4}\geq f_{3}\geq f_{2i} for all 1≤i≤a1\leq i\leq a and i≠2i\neq 2. Finally, deg+⁡v2=1\deg^{+}v_{2}=1 and v2v_{2} receives chips from v5v_{5}, so f4≥f3≥f2≥f5f_{4}\geq f_{3}\geq f_{2}\geq f_{5}.
∎
Using Lemma 3.14, we provide a factorial lower bound for the cc-convergent period of a useful Ka,aK_{a,\,a} orientation for sufficiently large cc.
Theorem 3.15.
For a useful orientation of Ka,aK_{a,\,a}, with c=a⁡(a−2)+1c=a(a-2)+1, we have Tc​(Ka,a)=Ω⁡(a!)T_{c}(K_{a,\,a})=\Omega(a!).
Proof.
Since the number of chips on each vertex does not change after a full period, the following equations must hold.
f2​a=(a−1)​f1\displaystyle f_{2a}=(a-1)f_{1}
(3.5)
f1+f5+f7+…+f2​a−1=f2\displaystyle f_{1}+f_{5}+f_{7}+\ldots+f_{2a-1}=f_{2}
f1+f2​j−1+f2​j+3+…+f2​a−1=(j−1)​f2​jfor2≤j≤a−2\displaystyle f_{1}+f_{2j-1}+f_{2j+3}+\ldots+f_{2a-1}=(j-1)f_{2j}\quad\text{for}\quad 2\leq j\leq a-2
f2​j−2+f2​j+2+f2​j+4+…+f2​a=(j−1)​f2​j−1for2≤j≤a−1\displaystyle f_{2j-2}+f_{2j+2}+f_{2j+4}+\ldots+f_{2a}=(j-1)f_{2j-1}\quad\text{for}\quad 2\leq j\leq a-1
f1+f2​a−3=(a−2)​f2​a−2\displaystyle f_{1}+f_{2a-3}=(a-2)f_{2a-2}
f2​a−2=(a−1)​f2​a−1\displaystyle f_{2a-2}=(a-1)f_{2a-1}
f2​a−1=(a−1)​f2​a\displaystyle f_{2a-1}=(a-1)f_{2a}
For any solution, scaling gives a new solution, so System 3.5 is not independent. We arbitrarily drop the first equation, leaving a system of 2​a−12a-1 independent equations in 2​a2a variables. These remaining equations are linearly independent, as can be seen later in the un-augmented form of Matrix 3.8, whose rank is equal to the number of rows.
By definition, each of f1,f2,…,f2​a≥0.f_{1},f_{2},\ldots,\,f_{2a}\geq 0. By Lemma 2.2, if any fi=0f_{i}=0, we have that T=1T=1 and that all vertices are forever passive. However, the largest possible cc for which all vertices can be forever passive is ∑v(deg+⁡v−1)=|E|−|V|=a⁡(a−2)<c\sum_{v}(\deg^{+}v-1)=|E|-|V|=a(a-2)<c. Thus, we further constrain fi>0f_{i}>0 for all ii. Note that for any solution (f1,f2,…,f2​a)(f_{1},f_{2},\ldots,\,f_{2a}), it must be true that (λ​f1,λ​f2,…,λ​f2​a)(\lambda f_{1},\lambda f_{2},\ldots,\,\lambda f_{2a}) is also a solution. To find the minimal solution, we set f1=1f_{1}=1. System 3.5 has now been reduced to
f2−f5−f7−…−f2​a−1=1\displaystyle f_{2}-f_{5}-f_{7}-\ldots-f_{2a-1}=1
(3.6)
−f2​j−1+(j−1)​f2​j−f2​j+3−…−f2​a−1=1for2≤j≤a−2\displaystyle-f_{2j-1}+(j-1)f_{2j}-f_{2j+3}-\ldots-f_{2a-1}=1\quad\text{for}\quad 2\leq j\leq a-2
