Tc​(Ka,a)≥Ta=Ω⁡(a!).T_{c}(K_{a,\,a})\geq T_{a}=\Omega(a!).
Remark 3.16.
Matrix 3.7 is extremely similar to the out-degree Laplacian matrix of Ka,aK_{a,\,a}. As in the case of the complete graph, the matrix representation of 3.5 is exactly the out-degree Laplacian.
Now consider a complete bipartite graph Ka,bK_{a,b} with partitions LL and RR, such that |L|=a|L|=a and |R|=b|R|=b with a≤ba\leq b. Let vi∈Lv_{i}\in L for 1≤i≤a1\leq i\leq a and vi∈Rv_{i}\in R for a+1≤i≤a+ba+1\leq i\leq a+b. Define an orientation of Ka,bK_{a,b} to be useful if the following statements hold.
•
The induced subgraph defined by vertices viv_{i} for 1≤i≤2​a1\leq i\leq 2a is a useful orientation of Ka,aK_{a,a}.
•
The induced subgraph defined by vertices viv_{i} for 1≤i≤2​a1\leq i\leq 2a is the only sink component.
Corollary 3.17.
For the useful orientation of Ka,bK_{a,b}, with c=(b−a)​(a−1)+a⁡(a−2)+1c=(b-a)(a-1)+a(a-2)+1, we have Tc​(Ka,b)=Ω⁡(a!)T_{c}(K_{a,\,b})=\Omega(a!).
Proof.
By Proposition 2.1, no vertices viv_{i} with i>2​ai>2a fire in a parallel chip-firing game on Ka,bK_{a,\,b} so those b−ab-a vertices will have at most a−1a-1 chips each. Then the period of the game is simply the period of the induced subgraph Ka,aK_{a,a}, with c′≥a⁡(a−2)+1c^{\prime}\geq a(a-2)+1 chips on it. Thus, Tc​(Ka,b)=Tc′​(Ka,a)=Ω⁡(a!)T_{c}(K_{a,b})=T_{c^{\prime}}(K_{a,a})=\Omega(a!).
∎
4 Atomic Firing Sequences
Let the firing sequence of a vertex vv in a parallel chip-firing game on a directed graph be the infinite binary string F0​(v)​F1​(v)​…F_{0}(v)F_{1}(v)\ldots. Let the length-ℓ\ell firing sequence of a vertex vv be the binary string F0​(v)​F1​(v)​…​Fℓ−1​(v)F_{0}(v)F_{1}(v)\ldots F_{\ell-1}(v). Let the atomic firing sequence of a vertex vv be the length-TT firing sequence of vv.
We next define some properties of binary strings. Let s=s0​s1​…​sℓ−1s=s_{0}s_{1}\ldots s_{\ell-1} be a length-ℓ\ell binary string. A binary string is periodic with period kk, with 1≤k≤⌊ℓ2⌋1\leq k\leq\lfloor\frac{\ell}{2}\rfloor, if it can be formed by concatenating copies of a length-kk binary string. We first show that no binary string of length ℓ≥2\ell\geq 2 consisting of only “0”s can be the atomic firing sequence of a vertex in a parallel chip-firing game on a strongly connected directed graph.
Lemma 4.1.
For any binary string ss of length ℓ≥2\ell\geq 2 consisting of only “0”s, no vertex in any parallel chip-firing game on a strongly connected directed graph has ss as its atomic firing sequence.
Proof.
If a vertex had ss as its atomic firing sequence, it would be forever passive and we would have T=ℓ≥2T=\ell\geq 2. However, by Lemma 2.2, each vertex must then fire at least once in each period, so no such vertex exists.
∎
We have now established a set of binary strings that cannot be the atomic firing sequence of a vertex in a parallel chip-firing game. We next show that all other binary strings can be atomic firing sequences. First, we prove that all other binary strings of length ℓ≤2\ell\leq 2 can be atomic firing sequences.
Lemma 4.2.
For any binary string ss of length 1 or 2 excluding s=00s=00, there exists a parallel chip-firing game on a directed graph such that some vertex vv has ss as its atomic firing sequence.
Proof.
If s=0s=0, any vertex in any parallel chip-firing game with no chips will have ss as its atomic firing sequence. Similarly, if s=1s=1, we can construct the C3C_{3} graph, for example, with one chip on each vertex. Then every vertex fires on every round, so every vertex again has ss as its atomic firing sequence.
If s=01s=01 or s=10s=10, we can construct the C4C_{4} graph and place one chip each on v0v_{0} and v2v_{2}. Then v0v_{0} and v2v_{2} have atomic firing sequence 1010 and v1v_{1} and v3v_{3} have atomic firing sequence 0101. Finally, if s=11s=11, we construct a graph DD with 4 vertices and edge set E={(v1,v2),(v2,v3),(v2,v4),(v3,v1),(v4,v1)}E=\{(v_{1},\,v_{2}),\,(v_{2},\,v_{3}),\,(v_{2},\,v_{4}),\,(v_{3},\,v_{1}),\,(v_{4},\,v_{1})\} (see Figure 6). The parallel chip-firing game on DD with c0​(v1)=c0​(v2)=2c_{0}(v_{1})=c_{0}(v_{2})=2 and c0​(v3)=c0​(v4)=0c_{0}(v_{3})=c_{0}(v_{4})=0 has T=2T=2, and v1v_{1} fires on both rounds. Thus, v1v_{1} has atomic firing sequence s=11s=11.
∎
12342200
12341111
Figure 6: The final parallel chip-firing game from Lemma 4.2. Vertex 1 has atomic firing sequence 1111.
Lemma 4.2 concerns very short binary strings, for which our main theorem does not hold. However, all longer binary strings that contain at least one 1 can also be the atomic firing sequences of a vertex in a parallel chip-firing game on a directed graph. The following lemma constructs a parallel chip-firing game on a directed cycle in which a vertex has an arbitrary binary string ss of length ℓ≥3\ell\geq 3 as its length-ℓ\ell firing sequence.
Lemma 4.3.
For any binary string ss of length ℓ≥3\ell\geq 3, there exists a parallel chip-firing game on a directed graph such that a vertex vv has ss as its length-ℓ\ell firing sequence. If ss is not periodic, the atomic firing sequence of vv is its length-ℓ\ell firing sequence.
Proof.
Consider the simple cycle CℓC_{\ell}. Let c0​(vi)=sic_{0}(v_{i})=s_{i}. On each round, all vertices with a chip fire, so the chips shift one vertex along the cycle. Therefore, ct​(v0)=stc_{t}(v_{0})=s_{t}. Since ct​(v0)=Ft​(v0)c_{t}(v_{0})=F_{t}(v_{0}), we have that ss is the length-ℓ\ell firing sequence of v0v_{0}.
Note that if ss is not periodic, the first round TT where cT​(vi)=c0​(vi)=sic_{T}(v_{i})=c_{0}(v_{i})=s_{i} is T=ℓT=\ell. In this case, the atomic firing sequence of v0v_{0} is its length-ℓ\ell firing sequence ss.
∎
Example 4.4.
In Figure 7, q0=s=11000=c0​(v1)​c0​(v2)​c0​(v3)​c0​(v4)​c0​(v5)q^{0}=s=11000=c_{0}(v_{1})c_{0}(v_{2})c_{0}(v_{3})c_{0}(v_{4})c_{0}(v_{5}). Since s=11000s=11000 is not periodic, T=5T=5.
0432111000
1543210100
15432001101543200011
1543201001
Figure 7: An example of a parallel chip-firing game from Lemma 4.3. Here s=11000s=11000 is not periodic, and v0v_{0} has ss as its atomic firing sequence.
Lemma 4.3 fails to construct a parallel chip-firing game in which a vertex has ss as its atomic firing sequence if ss is periodic with period kk, in which case the construction yields a parallel chip-firing game with T=k<ℓT=k<\ell. To show that a periodic binary string can be the atomic firing sequence of a vertex, we turn to our main theorem.
Let ℓ⁡(s)\ell(s) denote the length of a binary string ss. Let n⁡(s)n(s) denote the number of 1s in ss. Furthermore, let k⁡(s)k(s) with 0≤k⁡(s)≤ℓ⁡(s)−10\leq k(s)\leq\ell(s)-1 be the maximum value such that sk⁡(s)=1s_{k(s)}=1. Let d⁡(s)=ℓ⁡(s)−1−k⁡(s)d(s)=\ell(s)-1-k(s) be the number of trailing zeros in ss.
Example 4.5.
If s=1010s=1010, then n⁡(s)=2n(s)=2 and k⁡(s)=2k(s)=2 and d⁡(s)=1d(s)=1. If s=111000s=111000, then n⁡(s)=3n(s)=3 and k⁡(s)=2k(s)=2 and d⁡(s)=3d(s)=3.
In the following proof, we augment the construction from Lemma 4.3 to construct a parallel chip-firing game on a directed graph in which a vertex has a periodic atomic firing sequence. To do so, we draw inspiration from Jiang, Scully, and Zhang’s Jiang et al. (2015) proof of Theorem 4.1 and construct a parallel chip-firing game by attaching many copies of a graph to a single vertex.
Theorem 4.6.
For any binary string ss that contains 1, there exists a parallel chip-firing game on a directed graph such that ss is the firing sequence of some vertex vv.
Proof.
For brevity, we let ℓ=ℓ⁡(s)\ell=\ell(s) and n=n⁡(s)n=n(s). We also let k=k⁡(s)k=k(s) and d=d⁡(s)d=d(s). If ℓ≤2\ell\leq 2, we refer to Lemma 4.2.
We create 2​n2n copies of the simple cycle CℓC_{\ell} and label them C0,C1,…,C2​n−1C^{0},\,C^{1},\,\ldots,\,C^{2n-1}. We denote the iith vertex in CjC^{j} as vijv_{i}^{j}. We assign an initial configuration of chips in each cycle in the same way as in Lemma 4.3: c0​(vij)=sic_{0}(v_{i}^{j})=s_{i}. Additionally, we replace all the v0jv_{0}^{j} with a single vertex vv. Thus, there exist edges directed from v1jv_{1}^{j} to vv and from vv to vℓ−1jv_{\ell-1}^{j} for all jj. Also, vv has 0 chips if s0=0s_{0}=0, and vv has 2​n2n chips if s0=1s_{0}=1.
Since c0​(vij)c_{0}(v_{i}^{j}) is independent of jj, it follows that ct​(vij)c_{t}(v_{i}^{j}) is independent of jj for all tt. Thus, collecting all the v0jv_{0}^{j} into one vertex does not change Ft​(vij)F_{t}(v_{i}^{j}) for any tt or vijv_{i}^{j}. By Lemma 4.3, vv has ss as its length-ℓ\ell firing sequence. We now augment the graph to force TT to be ℓ\ell, regardless of whether ss is periodic.
We add a new vertex, uu, with an edge directed from vv to uu. We also add nn directed paths of dd vertices each. We will call these paths waterfalls. If d=0d=0, we instead add nn single vertices (as if d=1d=1). We label the iith vertex in the jjth waterfall uiju_{i}^{j}, where 0≤i≤d−10\leq i\leq d-1 and 0≤j≤n−10\leq j\leq n-1. We add edges directed from uu to u0ju_{0}^{j} and from ud−1ju_{d-1}^{j} to vv for all jj.
If d=0d=0, we set c0​(u)=nc_{0}(u)=n and c0​(u0j)=0c_{0}(u_{0}^{j})=0 for all jj. If d≥1d\geq 1, we instead set c0​(ud−1j)=1c_{0}(u_{d-1}^{j})=1 and c0​(u)=c0​(uij)=0c_{0}(u)=c_{0}(u_{i}^{j})=0 for all i≤d−2i\leq d-2 and all jj. In both cases, we also place nn more chips onto vv, so that c0​(v)=s0​(2​n)+nc_{0}(v)=s_{0}(2n)+n.
If d≥1d\geq 1, on the first round of the game, each vertex ud−1ju_{d-1}^{j} fires its 1 chip to vv. This will give vv a total of 2​n2n chips, ignoring the behavior of the cycles. Since deg+⁡v=2​n+1\deg^{+}v=2n+1, we see that vv will only have enough chips to fire when st=1s_{t}=1, so it will still have ss as its length-ℓ\ell firing sequence. On round k=ℓ−d−1k=\ell-d-1, vertex vv will fire for the nnth time, so that ck+1​(u)=n=deg+⁡uc_{k+1}(u)=n=\deg^{+}u. Then uu will fire one chip to each u0ju_{0}^{j} on round k+1k+1. Over the next d−1d-1 rounds, these nn chips will flow down the waterfalls. Then on round ℓ\ell, we will have ct​(uij)=c0​(uij)=1c_{t}(u_{i}^{j})=c_{0}(u_{i}^{j})=1. We also have cℓ​(vij)=c0​(vij)c_{\ell}(v_{i}^{j})=c_{0}(v_{i}^{j}) and cℓ​(v)=c0​(v)c_{\ell}(v)=c_{0}(v) by the cyclic construction of the vijv_{i}^{j}. Finally, cℓ​(u)=c0​(u)=0c_{\ell}(u)=c_{0}(u)=0. Thus, we have T=ℓT=\ell, so vv has ss as its atomic firing sequence.
If d=0d=0, the game proceeds almost identically. However, k=ℓ−1k=\ell-1, so cℓ​(u)=c0​(u)=nc_{\ell}(u)=c_{0}(u)=n. It can be verified that all other vertices have the same number of chips on round ℓ\ell as on round 0. Then we again have T=ℓT=\ell, so vv has ss as its atomic firing sequence.
∎
Example 4.7.
Figure 8 shows an example construction for s=1010s=1010. Note that n=n⁡(s)=2n=n(s)=2 and d=d⁡(s)=1d=d(s)=1. Chip numbers written beside the vertices in the outermost cycle apply to the corresponding vertices in each cycle. As shown, c0​(u00)=c0​(u01)=1c_{0}(u_{0}^{0})=c_{0}(u_{0}^{1})=1 and c0​(u)=0c_{0}(u)=0. Also, c0​(v)=s0​(2​n)+n=6c_{0}(v)=s_{0}(2n)+n=6. Since vv fires on rounds 0 and 2, we have k=2k=2, so uu fires on round 3. Then c4​(u00)=c4​(u01)=1c_{4}(u_{0}^{0})=c_{4}(u_{0}^{1})=1, so T=4T=4.
