6 T=2T=2 Games on Undirected Graphs
Lemma 6.1.
For any finite, connected, undirected graph G=(V,E)G=(V,\,E) with |V|≥2|V|\geq 2, there exists a parallel chip-firing game on GG with T=2T=2.
Proof.
We set up a parallel chip-firing game on GG in the following manner. We arbitrarily choose a vertex v∈Vv\in V and place deg⁡v\deg v chips onto it. For all other vertices uu, we use the following process.
•
If d⁡(u,v)d(u,\,v) is even, we place deg⁡u\deg u chips onto uu.
•
If d⁡(u,v)d(u,\,v) is odd, we place deg⁡u−n\deg u-n chips onto uu, where nn is the number of edges (u,w)∈E(u,\,w)\in E such that d⁡(u,v)≠d⁡(w,v)d(u,\,v)\neq d(w,\,v).
On the first round of the game, a vertex ν\nu fires if and only if d⁡(ν,v)d(\nu,\,v) is even. On the second round, all vertices that previously fired have lost chips and thus wait. However, the vertices that previously waited now have exactly enough chips to fire. Thus, all vertices fire exactly once in the first two rounds. By Lemma 3.6, T=2T=2.
∎
